WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040 align:start position:0%
 
you  there's been a boom in mega churches  with thousands of members over the past  two decades but that massive growth has  come with a cost the survey of pastors  shows the demands of the jobs are  creating greater stress and some pastors  are just feeling overwhelmed and even  burned out CBS News special  correspondent James Brown is here with  the story of a popular young pastor who  decided to just take a break two years  ago we met pastor Howard John Wesley the  dynamic leader of the historic Alpha  Street Baptist Church in Alexandria  Virginia he's a gifted preacher teacher  who uses popular culture in his messages  to young people but he told his 10,000  member congregation that he's tired  there's not been a day in these past  eleven years that I have not woken up  and knew that there's something I had to  do for the church that I have to be  available for call that that our journey  with people through the highest and the  lows of life now I want you to hear me  clearly I'm not exhausted I'm tired  there's a difference with those words  Howard John Wesley announced that he is  taking a three and a half month  sabbatical from the church he loves I'm  just tired running a little bit on empty  and the distinction between tired and  burned out to you is a joy I've not lost  my joy there's just some of the fatigue  that comes along with it that requires  re plugging in and charging up so I can  continue to do what I think I've been  called to do  11 years ago pastor Wesley was called to  lead Alpha Street Baptist Church and he  has done it well church membership has  quadrupled to ten thousand during his  tenure but the demands some self-imposed  have taken a toll on the fourth  generation preacher did you feel  compelled to work 24/7 it's the only  model I've ever known I was raised by a  dad who God rest his soul told me never  turned down a preach engagement and Here  I am at 47 with 21 years in pastoring  and going maybe that wasn't the best  advice that collar that robe doesn't  grant you any immunity from mental  disease and depression and anxiety  preacher burnout has been an issue in  the Christian community according to a  2015 survey of 1500 evangelical pastors  by LifeWay research 54 percent of  pastors feel their role is frequently  overwhelming 33 percent feel isolated  but 71 percent of churches have no plan  for a pastor to receive a periodic  sabbatical what pushed me this year was  that Pastor Jared Wilson took his life  in September Jared Wilson the associate  pastor at the 10,000 member harvest  Christian Fellowship in California died  by suicide he was just 30 years old  there's an unhealthy addiction just  standing in that pulpit you can delude  yourself into thinking that that's what  life is all about  we have Wesley admits that he sees a  therapist regularly and he plans to use  his sabbatical to begin a healthy  lifestyle of exercise a plant-based diet  and most of all tending to his own need  for personal time with God the greatest  thing that's come from that sermon and  the sabbatical is receiving hundreds of  emails and texts from other pastors who  said thank you I need to do this as well  do you feel freed released absolutely  absolutely because I'm not ashamed free  that I don't have to be Superman I don't  have to be perfect I don't have to be  super anointed every time I walk in this  building that I can be Howard John and  not just pastor Tony the congregation  welcomes a sabbatical that the pastor  needs as a matter of fact it starts on  January 1st and pastor Wesley will be  back just before Easter and the  pressures of being a pastor are immense  indeed no matter how big the flock this  is a trend that we're unfortunately  seeing across the board in churches big  and small yeah it's such an important  job it's not the kind of job you can  take a mental or physical break from  unless of course you get a sabbatical  like this one JB thank you very much

